From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:21:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(web): bound the H.264 the decoders hold

WebCodecsH264Decoder kept every frame it gave a VideoDecoder until its
picture came out, with no limit on their number or size, and kept each
frame's access unit as well, although the decoder has its own copy (the
EncodedVideoChunk) and reading the picture back needs only the regions.
A server could stream access units that produce no picture, or one only
now and then. The stall check (no picture for 3 s) bounded that by time
alone, and ZGFX lets a small PDU expand into megabytes of H.264, so 3 s
can be a great deal of it.

A frame in a decoder now keeps only what reading its picture back
takes, and the decoders hold at most:

- 256 frames each. A decoder keeps up with its stream, so frames wait
  in it about as long as one takes to decode, and the server stops at
  its unacknowledged frames: 256 is over two seconds of 60 Hz AVC444
  (two access units a frame), and a frame that waits longer than 3 s
  fails the decoder anyway.
- 128 MiB of H.264 in all: over a second at the highest bitrate any
  H.264 level allows (level 6.2, High profile: 1,000 Mbit/s), where
  servers send a few to tens of Mbit/s.

Beyond either, the decoder fails as on a decoder error: the session
ends with the reason, and the application can reconnect without H.264.
